<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Runtime: **attached node** (install and setup are in the overview). Start the app with `devenv shell -- iex --name ae@127.0.0.1 --cookie tutorial -S mix phx.server` — the long name is on purpose: Livebook runs as a long-named node and cannot reach a short-named one. If port 4000 was busy and you started with `PORT=4001`, the `localhost:4000` URLs below move with it.

## Why an agent needs more than grep

From here on the tutorial gets dense: events, projections, rules, decisions, processes. You will almost certainly pair with a coding agent through it. This lesson teaches you to make that agent **ask the system** instead of guessing, and to debug the way Ash wants you to: by reading the declared contract first, then the running state, and only then the code.

An Ash action is not a function. Try it: there is no `def complete` anywhere for a `:complete` action. A language server that searches for the symbol correctly returns nothing. An agent that greps `def create` and guesses is doing what a new hire does on day one.

So we give the agent **three instruments**, each answering a different question:

| Question | Instrument | Reads |
|---|---|---|
| "What does this action accept? Why was I forbidden? Which rule/decision/process applies?" | `ash_agent_tools` (`mix ash_agent.*`, MCP `ash-agent`) | the compiled Spark DSL |
| "What is the value right now? What does this code return?" | Tidewave `project_eval` / this Livebook | the **running** VM |
| "Where is this function, who calls it, rename it safely" | Serena over **Expert** (the Elixir LSP) | Elixir source |

They are complementary by structure: the language server reads Elixir and cannot read Ash DSL blocks (they are opaque text to it); the introspection layer reads Ash and does not see function bodies.

## Wiring it up

`ash_enterprise/.mcp.json` already declares the project servers:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "tidewave":   { "type": "http", "url": "http://localhost:4000/tidewave/mcp" },
    "ash_ai_dev": { "type": "http", "url": "http://localhost:4000/ash_ai/mcp" },
    "ash-agent":  { "type": "http", "url": "http://127.0.0.1:4100" }
  }
}
```

* **tidewave** and **ash_ai_dev** live inside the running dev server on :4000. They show as disconnected until that server is up.
* **ash-agent** is a *separate* VM: `mix ash_agent.serve`. It is compile-only (your app is never started: no Oban queues draining, no projectors running), loopback only on `127.0.0.1:4100`, and recompiles on file change. Do **not** run it inside the dev server's VM.
* **Serena** is optional and not configured in the repo. If you use it, point its Elixir backend at Expert through `.serena/project.yml`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```yaml
# .serena/project.yml
languages: [elixir]
ls_specific_settings:
  elixir:
    ls_path: /path/to/expert   # the Expert build you use; Serena runs `expert --stdio`
```

Expert needs a compiled project and pays a ~10 s cross-file index wait once; after that, reference lookups are tens of milliseconds.

Finally, make sure the agent has read the rules it will be judged by:

```bash
mix usage_rules.sync    # regenerates AGENTS.md from every ash_* package's usage-rules.md
```

## The facade: the same answers, in the VM

Everything the Mix tasks and MCP tools return is a plain map from `AshAgentTools`, so you can use it here directly. Nothing below executes an action.

```elixir
AshAgentTools.describe_resource(AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition)
|> Map.take([:name, :actions])
```

## Describe before you write

Ask for the exact input contract of an action: required, optional and private keys with normalized types, plus the source location.

Describe the `:create` action of `AshEnterprise.Accounts.Organization`. (`AshEnterprise.Contracts` cannot give you one: it is read-only by design — describe it with `describe_resource/1` and look at its actions list. `AshAgentTools.list_resources/0` is how you find candidates.)

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  resource =
    AshAgentTools.list_resources()
    |> Enum.find(&(&1 == AshEnterprise.Accounts.Organization))

  AshAgentTools.describe_action(resource, :create)
  ```

  The shell equivalent is `mix ash_agent.describe <Resource> create`.

  </div>
</details>

## Validate before you act

`validate_input/3` casts each param with the same `Ash.Type.cast_input/3` machinery Ash uses at runtime, against a changeset built with `error?: false`. The data layer is never touched. The report is always returned, never raised; check `valid?`.

Validate a deliberately broken call to the same action: a camelCase key and a malformed UUID. Read the `errors` and any `did_you_mean`.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  report =
    AshAgentTools.validate_input(resource, :create, %{
      "organizationId" => "not-a-uuid"
    })

  {report.valid?, report.errors}
  ```

  This is the loop you want an agent in: validate, read the error, fix the input, validate again, *then* call the code interface. Shell: `mix ash_agent.validate <Resource> create '{"organizationId": "x"}'`.

  </div>
</details>

## Debugging "forbidden"

The most common Ash debugging session is "why was I forbidden?". Do it in two steps, and do not skip the second.

1. **What can deny?** `explain_forbidden/2` lists the policies that can forbid an action, in readable form. It lists; it does not rule.
2. **What is the verdict for this actor?** `can/4` evaluates the policies for a real actor and returns a verdict, without running the action. It wraps `Ash.can?/3`.

```elixir
AshAgentTools.explain_forbidden(AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition, :publish)
```

A fresh database has zero users, so make an actor first — `register_with_password` is a real create action on `AshEnterprise.Accounts.User` (find-or-create, so the cell re-runs). Then ask for the verdict on a **read** of the same resource.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  require Ash.Query

  email = "you@tutorial.example"

  actor =
    AshEnterprise.Accounts.User
    |> Ash.Query.filter(email == ^email)
    |> Ash.read_one!(authorize?: false) ||
      Ash.create!(
        Ash.Changeset.for_create(
          AshEnterprise.Accounts.User,
          :register_with_password,
          %{email: email, password: "tutorial1234", password_confirmation: "tutorial1234"},
          authorize?: false
        )
      )

  AshAgentTools.can(AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition, :read, actor)
  ```

  Why a read? `can/3` for an **update or destroy** action with no `record:` option evaluates the action against the actor's *own record* — so `can(Definition, :publish, actor)` asks "can this user publish *themselves*?" and fails with `no such update action on AshEnterprise.Accounts.User: :publish`. Pass `record:` to target a real record, or ask about a read, which needs no target. Lesson 15 will give this actor roles and privileges; for now `{:ok, false}` with an `explain_forbidden/2` listing is exactly the expected answer.

  Shell: `mix ash_agent.can AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition read --actor AshEnterprise.Accounts.User:<id>`.

  Note what you just did: `authorize?: false` in a notebook is debugging. In `lib/`, the enterprise packages fail the build if a second `authorize?: false` appears. Engine calls go through each package's `Scope` instead.

  </div>
</details>

## Debugging with the declared layers

Later lessons add layers that are hard to debug by reading code, because the behaviour is data. The facade reads each of them, read-only, and only when the package is present:

| Layer | Call | Shell |
|---|---|---|
| State machine | `AshAgentTools.transitions(Resource)` | `mix ash_agent.transitions Resource` (with Mermaid) |
| Rules (17) | `AshAgentTools.evaluate_rules(Module, facts)` | `mix ash_agent.rules --facts ...` |
| Decisions (19) | `AshAgentTools.decisions/0`, `decision_evaluate(key, inputs)` | `mix ash_agent.evaluate key '{...}'`, a dry run that records nothing |
| Processes (21) | `processes/0`, `process_graph(key)`, `process_instance(definition_key: ...)` | `mix ash_agent.instance --key k` shows where a token is stuck |
| Runtime | (boots the app on purpose) | `mix ash_agent.runtime snapshot`, `top 20`, `tree AshEnterprise` |

List the decision catalogue now. It may be empty until lesson 19 publishes one; that is an answer too.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  AshAgentTools.decisions()
  ```

  </div>
</details>

## Where am I? Context at a cursor

When an agent (or you) is looking at line 42 of a file, `context/3` says what Ash entity lives there: which resource, which section, which action or policy. `resolve/1` goes the other way, from a **name path** to a span with a digest. A name path is `Module`, or `Module/dsl_path/name` — one segment level, so name the action too:

```elixir
AshAgentTools.resolve("AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition/actions/publish")
```

`.../actions` alone is malformed — the grammar is `Module` or `Module/dsl_path/name[index]` (unnamed entities like policies are addressed by occurrence: `.../policies/policy[0]`). Serena addresses `Module/function`; `ash_agent_tools` addresses `Resource/section/entity`.

## Editing the DSL safely

Agents corrupt DSL blocks by text-patching them. `mix ash_agent.edit` (and `AshAgentTools.Edit`) edits by name path instead, with a **read-before-edit digest handshake**: you must present the digest you read, so an edit against a stale view is refused. After writing, it compiles and validates, and **reverts on failure**. It is dry-run by default.

```bash
mix ash_agent.edit replace AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund/actions/approve --file new_block.ex      # dry run
mix ash_agent.edit replace AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund/actions/approve --file new_block.ex --apply
```

Use Serena for Elixir symbols (rename a function, find callers of a module); use `ash_agent.edit` for DSL entities. Neither replaces the other.

## Live code: evaluate, do not speculate

The running VM is the truth about values. Tidewave's `project_eval` (or this attached Livebook) runs code in the app:

```elixir
# What is the active Oban config? What projectors are registered? Ask, don't grep.
{Application.get_env(:ash_enterprise, Oban) |> Keyword.take([:queues]),
 Application.get_env(:ash_events_projections, :projectors)}
```

A debugging loop that works, in order:

1. **Contract**: `describe_action` / `validate_input`. Most "bugs" are inputs.
2. **Authorization**: `explain_forbidden`, then `can` with the real actor.
3. **Declared behaviour**: transitions, rules, decision dry-run, process instance.
4. **Running state**: `project_eval`, `mix ash_agent.runtime`, the event log (lesson 15 onward). Read the events before reading the code: they tell you which action actually ran, by whom, with what.
5. **Code**: only now, Serena `find_symbol` / references over Expert, and `dbg/1` or `IEx.pry` in a change module.
6. **Prove it**: a failing test, then the fix.

```elixir
# dbg/1 inside a pipeline shows every intermediate step
AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition
|> Ash.Query.limit(1)
|> dbg()
|> Ash.read!(authorize?: false)
```

## Judge before you claim done

The **26 Iron Laws** are checked by a deterministic, grep-tier judge: no LLM, no keys, no compile. It reports violations only, at three tiers: `definite`, `likely`, `review`.

```bash
git diff main | mix ash_agent.laws - --diff
mix ash_agent.laws lib/ash_enterprise_web/live/dashboard_live.ex
```

Judge this deliberately bad LiveView. Before running it, predict which laws fire.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir
code = """
defmodule BadLive do
  use Phoenix.LiveView
  def mount(_p, _s, socket) do
    Phoenix.PubSub.subscribe(MyApp.PubSub, "tickets")
    {:ok, assign(socket, tickets: Ash.read!(MyApp.Ticket), kind: String.to_atom("x"))}
  end
end
"""
```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  AshAgentTools.judge_laws(code)
  ```

  Expect **three** violations: the query in `mount/3` (law 1 — it runs twice, once for the dead render and once on connect), `subscribe` without `connected?/1` (law 3), and `String.to_atom/1` (law 10). The fourth sin — assigning the list instead of streaming it — is law 2, and it is *behavioral*: the judge has no detector for it, so it fires nothing and you have to spot it yourself. You fixed all four by hand in lessons 10 and 12; the judge catches three of them. "Grep-tier" means read each hit's context before acting.

  </div>
</details>

## Closing the loop: kaizen

When a tool misses (unknown input, search miss, context miss), `ash_agent_tools` emits `[:ash_agent, :tool_gap]` telemetry with `did_you_mean` candidates. `mix ash_agent.gaps` turns the aggregate into a worklist of aliases and doc fixes. Treat recurring agent confusion as a defect in your declarations or docs, not in the agent.

## The rule for the rest of the tutorial

> **Ask the declaration, then the running system, then the code.**

In every lesson that follows, when something surprises you, describe it, validate it, dry-run it, read its events, and only then open the source.

